nning a piece of  code but for example if we take that  factorial function that I had on the  first slide so here's the dot ll file  that contains that code well when we  extract the executable we can take our  [ __ ] code extracted Oh camel compile it  as an interpreter and then run our  interpreter on this particular thing  which you'll see it's calling factorial  of 5 and we get 120 which is what you  would expect so cool we can we can use  this to write an LVM interpreter but we  can do more right so what can we do with  that well we can find bugs in the  existing LVM interpreters by comparing  them or we also need to know that what  our semantics is is that agrees with  other LLVM semantics so that's a hard  problem now we can use tools like quick  chick which is an automatic test  generation facility to try to find bugs  in our interpreter or LLVM semantics  this way and for the the most important  thing is we can try to use the semantics  to validate program transformations by  proving them correct in Cocke and  eventually maybe we could do a fully  verified compiler so again I don't have  a lot of time to go into like what it is  like to prove things in Cocke but here's  a little example of a proof this is I  picked this one because it's small and  it fits on the slide it's not very  interesting it's just saying something  about renaming the identifiers in your  program it says if you consistently  rename all the identifiers in an integer  operation and then you could have done  that before after you've taken the step  of computation so it's kind of a  fundamental renaming lemma  the word alpha conversion means  something to you then this is a proof of  that fact for a bit of our language but  the main point is that just like you can  define functions in Cocke you can define  lemmas and you can interactively work  with a clock infrastructure to prove  those lemmas about the program and we've  done that for many different kinds of  optimizations soft bound as what got us  into this originally we were looking at  doing memory safety some of the  interesting optimization passes of LVM  revolve around memory register promotion  which is called the mem to reg pass and  there are various analyses that you can  prove correct with this kind of this  kind of technique so I want to just  briefly sketch what that looks like at a  high level and I'm running out of time  but so for this mem to reg optimization  pass this is a register promotion pass  in the compiler it basically takes  programs without SSA form or sort of an  a trivial SSA form and introduces those  fee notes that I mentioned at the  beginning and that's very important for  getting good performance because it  enables register allocation and sort of  many downstream optimizations and just  to give you a flavor of what it looks  like here's a little bit of C code here  is sort of a little LLVM snippet that  you'll notice uses some loads and stores  to memory to model the c variable X  because that's what you would do but an  efficient version of this doesn't need  to put X in memory it can put it in a  register that's basically the  optimization that's going on and so  mentor edge takes that those loads and  stores etc and removes them by  introducing temporary registers in the  appropriate places and so this is a  fairly complicated algorithm and we're  able to prove a variant of it correct by  sort of breaking it down into a whole  pipeline of optimizations that these  acronyms here stand for things like dead  store elimination and there's sort of  the fundamental building blocks for  doing interesting optimizations of LVM  code and the point is that with a little  bit of work we can express in Cocke  these aliasing properties can prove each  of these little orange bubbles correct  for example to use various lemmas about  dead instruction elimination and so on  and then connect it all together to get  a theorem that you can write down  formally in coq but I've transliterated  here as you know this reg register  allocation algorithm preserves the  semantics of the source program okay and  then we can extract that implementation  so we had an implementation of this  algorithm and coq we can extract it plug  it into the LLVM compiler and run it  against real C code because it's just  another LVM pass like any other and we  can even then compare the result against  the other mem the existing mem - reg so  by doing this we don't lose any  performance because it's essentially the  same algorithm we've just verified it  and now we know that there are no bugs  and and we're confident that this is  correct we can there's a interesting  stories about differences between the  sort of existing LLVM infrastructure and  our version that I'm happy to take  offline ok so that's that's all great  what about some caveats this is there's  no free lunch right so verification of  this kind is very labor-intensive and I  would say we still don't have a good you  know handle on dealing with verification  at this scale the performance of the  resulting code as we just saw in that  little graph might be the same but the  performance of the compiler itself  because we're building it in Cocke and  extracting it is not as good as the c++  LVM implementations so that's problem  and one issue with formalization of any  kind is your sort of pinned to a  particular semantics and the LLVM is a  changing target so what have we done  about that well for this first thing I  think there's a lot of progress being  made in this deep spec project and  elsewhere about how to do proof  engineering I haven't talked about  automation and tactics and various  libraries and so on that you can use to  make all this more palatable for  performance well that's  open challenge I think you could use the  unverified version of things if you want  to just test and debug and then plug in  the expensive compiler pass that you  know is correct from the final release  or there are other technical approaches  like translation validation that try to  get the best of both worlds  and for this LVM moving target thing  well this is one of the things we  struggle with there's a-there adding  features to LVM all the time and we  can't really keep up so what are we  doing well we've been re-engineering the  velum project sort of from scratch  starting a couple of years ago as this  this deep spec project and to do that  we've introduced a bunch of libraries  that we hope can be useful in other  contexts for doing with that and we're  experimenting with better automation to  make all this work better ok so I just  want to wrap up quickly by pointing you  to I mentioned already John riggers work  but there's a lot of other work in the  space of LLVM semantics and I've had a  lot of collaborations with some of the  people on the slides and you can take a  look at other interesting projects in  this area similarly I mentioned comp  search but there are also other tools  like the verified software tool chain  and if you're curious for those of you  who are still trying to or potentially  interested in getting into into caulk  itself I recommend my colleague Benjamin  Pierce's software foundation's textbook  which is a free online introduction to  caulk and you can there are four volumes  now that you can go up to interesting  data structures and once you've done  those you can take a look at Adams  Apollo's certified programming with  dependent types and if you're interested  more about deep spec or the velum  project you can find the vellum  development on our github account and  it's also linked to from the broader  deep spec or web pages where you can  read about all these other fascinating  projects that are applying caulk to  systems code so thank you  you
 

